1
00:00:03,645 --> 00:00:07,168
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any ideas, Prabhupāda, to sum up?

2
00:00:11,771 --> 00:00:19,536
[SPEAKER_04]: Maybe about the sankhāras and the difference between sankhāras and cetanā.

3
00:00:27,862 --> 00:00:29,103
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do you think is the difference?

4
00:00:31,617 --> 00:00:36,241
[SPEAKER_04]: Saṅkharas are involved with avijja.

5
00:00:39,945 --> 00:00:43,328
[SPEAKER_04]: Cetanas are more like free from avijja.

6
00:00:47,220 --> 00:00:51,461
[SPEAKER_00]: But isn't... can you have cetanā without it being sankhāra?

7
00:00:52,462 --> 00:00:59,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sankhāra just means activity or doing.

8
00:01:00,264 --> 00:01:09,167
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can you have intention or choice without it being an activity?

9
00:01:17,355 --> 00:01:29,003
[SPEAKER_04]: From my point of view, I cannot step outside of my experience as a whole and see how would it be without avijjā.

10
00:01:33,167 --> 00:01:37,628
[SPEAKER_03]: So, but what do you think it... like, what do you have to remove?

11
00:01:38,348 --> 00:01:41,389
[SPEAKER_00]: So, everybody recognizes that they have sankhāras in their experience.

12
00:01:42,290 --> 00:01:58,574
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, whether that's chetan or not, we can put that aside, but what do you need to remove in order for... Well, can we... but are we on the same page about what we are talking about when I used to do a sankhāra to begin with?

13
00:01:59,333 --> 00:01:59,513
[SPEAKER_03]: Right.

14
00:01:59,553 --> 00:02:01,353
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I guess we should verify that first.

15
00:02:01,393 --> 00:02:18,177
[SPEAKER_00]: So, usually, it's understood as like volitional formations or various kinds of translations that people have come up with and it usually feels to people, I think, like it's it's something that's not really my control.

16
00:02:19,277 --> 00:02:25,498
[SPEAKER_00]: It's something that gets removed kind of indirectly by this practice that I do that at some point

17
00:02:26,422 --> 00:02:30,244
[SPEAKER_00]: It gets rid of the ignorance and somehow also gets rid of the saṅkharas.

18
00:02:31,345 --> 00:02:37,108
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh... it's actually something that you are active... like it's a doing.

19
00:02:37,228 --> 00:02:38,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Saṅkhara means doing.

20
00:02:39,489 --> 00:02:41,990
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... in kind of a...

21
00:02:43,551 --> 00:02:51,135
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess, eh... these are not like philosophical terms that the Buddha was using with some kind of grandiose significance.

22
00:02:51,235 --> 00:02:54,737
[SPEAKER_00]: It's if you... if you read like Vedic writings and stuff, when they...

23
00:02:55,335 --> 00:03:14,287
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the use of the word saṅkhara, they were referring it to the stuff that they would do, like rituals, like things that they would organize, play out, kind of do, and not as some kind of, I don't know, energies or something.

24
00:03:15,108 --> 00:03:24,734
[SPEAKER_02]: So, saṅkharas have to be seen in the practical sense of things that you are doing, things that you're responsible for.

25
00:03:25,927 --> 00:03:38,317
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, from that point of view, what would you have to remove to fix the problem with santhāras, basically?

26
00:03:50,892 --> 00:03:53,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you just step out of saṅkharas from the very beginning?

27
00:03:54,457 --> 00:03:54,717
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

28
00:03:55,699 --> 00:03:58,122
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the thing that you have to do then?

29
00:03:59,824 --> 00:04:02,768
[SPEAKER_04]: Understand what saṅkhara are.

30
00:04:02,828 --> 00:04:04,210
[SPEAKER_04]: Right, but even before that?

31
00:04:13,063 --> 00:04:24,972
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, you have to recognize, if you think you can kind of avoid them from the very beginning, you probably have a misconception of what saṅkharas are.

32
00:04:25,752 --> 00:04:29,976
[SPEAKER_02]: All you can do is cultivate certain kinds of saṅkharas.

33
00:04:30,840 --> 00:04:32,901
[SPEAKER_02]: that don't fuel ignorance.

34
00:04:34,141 --> 00:04:37,163
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have no choice but to do things, in other words.

35
00:04:38,143 --> 00:04:42,685
[SPEAKER_02]: But, certain doings will, at least, not fuel your ignorance further.

36
00:04:43,566 --> 00:04:44,366
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's the first step.

37
00:04:44,426 --> 00:04:51,349
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to recognize that there are wholesome saṅkharas and unwholesome saṅkharas.

38
00:04:52,610 --> 00:04:53,530
[SPEAKER_00]: And that, yeah, so that's it.

39
00:04:53,690 --> 00:04:54,770
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the practice of virtue.

40
00:04:56,051 --> 00:04:58,072
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, on the basis of that,

41
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:02,782
[SPEAKER_00]: You can get to understand what the cessation of sankhāras actually is.

42
00:05:02,842 --> 00:05:14,350
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when you have established yourself in good sankhāras, quote-unquote, good actions, then you can understand the cessation of action altogether.

43
00:05:14,890 --> 00:05:18,492
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the real gist of it.

44
00:05:18,512 --> 00:05:28,159
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like... it's not that you remove ignorance and then you are left... you kind of go from sankhāras to cetanā.

45
00:05:29,079 --> 00:05:31,620
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, as the Buddha said, cetanā is kamma as well.

46
00:05:32,760 --> 00:05:36,481
[SPEAKER_02]: So, and he also said cessation of suffering is cessation of kamma.

47
00:05:37,562 --> 00:05:39,262
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, cessation of cetanā as well.

48
00:05:40,242 --> 00:05:51,326
[SPEAKER_02]: So, eh... more accurately speaking, eh... cessation of suffering is the cessation of all activities or all doings or all intentions as well.

49
00:05:52,066 --> 00:05:55,907
[SPEAKER_02]: And sometimes even the Buddha would say that eh... there is eh...

50
00:05:56,717 --> 00:06:02,844
[SPEAKER_02]: Intention, when there is cetanā, by body, speech and mind, as a result of that, suffering arises.

51
00:06:04,105 --> 00:06:10,191
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's the cessation of all of that, that constitutes actual freedom from suffering.

52
00:06:10,211 --> 00:06:14,896
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot have sankhāras or cetanā, and at the same time, not have suffering.

53
00:06:16,198 --> 00:06:22,883
[SPEAKER_02]: But, of course, now then, the thing is, it's not a... like, it can sound as if...

54
00:06:22,903 --> 00:06:33,532
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, so, cessation of all action probably will sound to most people as some kind of a exotic state of just unconsciousness or something.

55
00:06:34,273 --> 00:06:39,217
[SPEAKER_03]: But, it's something that you have to actually develop.

56
00:06:40,418 --> 00:06:42,980
[SPEAKER_03]: It's... and it's also...

57
00:06:45,357 --> 00:06:48,720
[SPEAKER_02]: Not something that happens to you, it's something that you understand.

58
00:06:49,160 --> 00:06:56,445
[SPEAKER_02]: So, literally, the word understanding refers to knowing something for yourself and knowing how you got there.

59
00:06:56,465 --> 00:07:05,192
[SPEAKER_03]: Not just, oh, I got this experience where there was no doer, no sense of self, but, okay, but what do you actually understand there?

60
00:07:05,872 --> 00:07:07,534
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what really matters.

61
00:07:10,216 --> 00:07:14,259
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how then would you

62
00:07:16,343 --> 00:07:22,567
[SPEAKER_03]: Or let's say, first of all, what is the ignorance as a result of which there are saṅkharas automatically?

63
00:07:26,969 --> 00:07:31,091
[SPEAKER_03]: What are you most acutely not aware of?

64
00:07:32,272 --> 00:07:42,358
[SPEAKER_02]: Instead of just tell the five aggregates or some kind of theoretical thing, what are you most acutely not aware of because of which you have saṅkharas?

65
00:07:49,132 --> 00:07:49,872
[SPEAKER_04]: Simultaneity.

66
00:07:50,812 --> 00:07:57,694
[SPEAKER_04]: There's something that undermines my attention itself.

67
00:08:01,855 --> 00:08:04,976
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, that would be all the practice, I think.

68
00:08:05,036 --> 00:08:14,418
[SPEAKER_02]: You could put it that way, but even before you can go in that direction, there's something more, even more acute, that you need to recognize.

69
00:08:16,679 --> 00:08:17,399
[SPEAKER_02]: Something very simple.

70
00:08:22,372 --> 00:08:31,361
[SPEAKER_03]: There's a characteristic of saṅkharas that's pretty much... like, it's the only reason why they can be there.

71
00:08:33,183 --> 00:08:35,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Take responsibility for my action.

72
00:08:36,186 --> 00:08:43,654
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, but how would you... what is the precondition for you to take responsibility for an action?

73
00:08:48,898 --> 00:08:49,819
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a very simple answer.

74
00:08:49,839 --> 00:08:50,379
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a precept.

75
00:08:51,040 --> 00:08:51,260
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

76
00:08:52,061 --> 00:08:55,944
[SPEAKER_03]: Very, very, very direct thing.

77
00:08:56,605 --> 00:09:05,052
[SPEAKER_03]: It's almost like a silly kind of question in a way, but you realize actually it's not.

78
00:09:06,613 --> 00:09:08,655
[SPEAKER_01]: Just be aware of myself.

79
00:09:09,622 --> 00:09:11,983
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, yeah, be aware that you're doing something to begin with.

80
00:09:12,243 --> 00:09:18,904
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that's the... like, it's not some unawareness of something.

81
00:09:19,304 --> 00:09:25,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, like, you can come up with all sorts of explanations of unawareness of three characteristics and this and that.

82
00:09:26,306 --> 00:09:33,928
[SPEAKER_03]: But really, most primarily, when it comes to saṅkharas, you're unaware of them to begin with.

83
00:09:34,608 --> 00:09:38,312
[SPEAKER_03]: In other words, you don't recognize that you're actually doing that thing.

84
00:09:39,092 --> 00:09:45,358
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yes, as you said, you... the first step to doing anything about it would be taking responsibility.

85
00:09:46,119 --> 00:09:51,063
[SPEAKER_03]: But in order to take responsibility for your action, you need to know that it is actually an action.

86
00:09:51,864 --> 00:09:53,786
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's not going to feel like that initially.

87
00:09:54,486 --> 00:10:00,392
[SPEAKER_02]: So, eh... because what we're talking about here is not really just, eh...

88
00:10:01,893 --> 00:10:07,057
[SPEAKER_02]: I can't hold some saṅkharas by body and speech, because, as we said, that would have to be abandoned beforehand.

89
00:10:07,917 --> 00:10:26,190
[SPEAKER_03]: So, these are now things that, having developed the good saṅkharas, as I said before, to replace the bad saṅkharas, you wouldn't even be aware that those saṅkharas are things that you're doing.

90
00:10:27,878 --> 00:10:30,161
[SPEAKER_05]: So, which includes mental action.

91
00:10:30,181 --> 00:10:30,581
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

92
00:10:30,701 --> 00:10:43,716
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, most primarily, it converges at that point, basically, because even when somebody goes into complete state of just vegetable or something, that's the part that can never go away.

93
00:10:44,037 --> 00:10:45,018
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

94
00:10:45,098 --> 00:10:46,920
[SPEAKER_02]: On some level, if you are alive,

95
00:10:48,481 --> 00:10:50,803
[SPEAKER_05]: There is some degree of activity.

96
00:10:50,843 --> 00:10:56,486
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's actually already a good contemplation, to just ask yourself, like, mental action.

97
00:10:56,826 --> 00:11:00,729
[SPEAKER_05]: So, instead of just agreeing, oh yeah, there's body, speech and mind, so there must be mental action.

98
00:11:00,789 --> 00:11:06,492
[SPEAKER_05]: But, can I make a list of things that I've been mentally doing, mentally doing in the

99
