00:18:38,439 --> 00:18:43,724
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, you could say, okay, well, it's only to the extent that the

176
00:18:48,364 --> 00:18:53,435
[SPEAKER_03]: Nothing that you would do would be rooted in... That's factually when you would have no self.

177
00:18:55,120 --> 00:19:02,403
[SPEAKER_03]: There's a sense of an outside kind of thing that's trying to puppet stuff around to kind of get what it wants.

178
00:19:03,143 --> 00:19:15,668
[SPEAKER_03]: But, see, you arrive at it indirectly, through that non-inclination, rather than, like, deciding beforehand that you will deny your sense of control, rooted in your sense of control, that just wants to get rid of things.

179
00:19:16,528 --> 00:19:23,631
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's... that sense of self, that sense of I, is a side effect

180
00:19:24,652 --> 00:19:36,169
[SPEAKER_00]: of that inclination to control things, rather than being some kind of metaphysical entity that you destroy or something.

181
00:19:45,445 --> 00:20:01,838
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, that's where you need to look for the saṅkharas, in relation to pleasure and pain, because that's where the activation, where the inclination comes to be.

182
00:20:03,440 --> 00:20:12,247
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in relation to the things that you want to do, that your mind is pressured to do, that's where you calm the saṅkharas, not in relation to kind of everywhere, nowhere.

183
00:20:14,058 --> 00:20:28,183
[SPEAKER_03]: It's when you have contained yourself by the precepts, you will naturally, whether you like it or not, unless you're distracting yourself with some kind of oblivion practice,

184
00:20:29,185 --> 00:20:33,269
[SPEAKER_02]: You will start to notice that your mind is inclining towards certain things.

185
00:20:34,590 --> 00:20:35,231
[SPEAKER_02]: It's inevitable.

186
00:20:36,252 --> 00:20:53,208
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where you need to then start understanding, okay, how do I cut this off at the root, rather than replacing it with something else, which is going to be another activity, or kind of thinking to counter it, or just looking away

187
00:20:54,715 --> 00:20:56,056
[SPEAKER_00]: All of those are going to be activities.

188
00:20:56,316 --> 00:21:07,041
[SPEAKER_03]: Instead, you need to find how do I go to the very root of this whole inclination, this whole movement of just trying.

189
00:21:07,962 --> 00:21:12,264
[SPEAKER_02]: Not trying to do something, just trying as a thing in itself.

190
00:21:13,004 --> 00:21:13,925
[SPEAKER_03]: How do I cut that off?

191
00:21:14,205 --> 00:21:18,667
[SPEAKER_03]: And, well, it's exactly through what you said initially.

192
00:21:19,728 --> 00:21:24,210
[SPEAKER_00]: It's through the understanding of dependent origination, quote-unquote.

193
00:21:27,300 --> 00:21:39,473
[SPEAKER_02]: By recognizing with this, this is, you kind of remove any space because of which you could assume that you can do something about it.

194
00:21:40,454 --> 00:21:43,197
[SPEAKER_02]: Because all you see is this and this other thing.

195
00:21:44,438 --> 00:21:46,941
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, where is the space for me to interfere in between?

196
00:21:56,979 --> 00:22:12,111
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why it's so important to see it as a simultaneous presence of two things, because for as long as it's like observing the one thing coming, and then the next one, and then the next one, it's going to be your sense of self that's observing that.

197
00:22:12,971 --> 00:22:22,279
[SPEAKER_02]: And that observation in and of itself is an activity, no matter how subtle it feels, like you thinking that you're seeing the sequence of mind moments coming and going.

198
00:22:22,819 --> 00:22:23,720
[SPEAKER_02]: That is an activity.

199
00:22:35,825 --> 00:22:52,214
[SPEAKER_00]: So, we gave one example of sankhāras, which is on a practical level, which would be daydreaming, let's say, and thinking coarse, sensual thoughts, perverse thoughts.

200
00:22:53,235 --> 00:23:02,980
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what would be another... can we come up with other examples of... so that it doesn't feel like an abstract thing, those sankhāras that you would be doing?

201
00:23:03,985 --> 00:23:10,889
[SPEAKER_00]: Even when you are already virtuous and established in a certain boundary of conduct.

202
00:23:20,275 --> 00:23:22,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Wanting something.

203
00:23:24,617 --> 00:23:26,618
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what would be the solution to that?

204
00:23:26,718 --> 00:23:29,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you just cash in wanting?

205
00:23:31,280 --> 00:23:32,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's become what I want now.

206
00:23:33,963 --> 00:23:35,445
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to start like that, of course.

207
00:23:35,705 --> 00:23:46,156
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, you won't... you won't try and you will just fall into some sort of abstract idea of, okay, I just don't want anything and that's it.

208
00:23:46,317 --> 00:23:49,520
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you have to start with an activity.

209
00:23:49,560 --> 00:23:52,163
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, I want to solve this because there's a problem here.

210
00:23:53,990 --> 00:23:55,811
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, how... so what would be the solution?

211
00:23:56,011 --> 00:24:18,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, how do you... Yeah, I think it's more, kind of, what you say, like, more or less, before, is, like, instead of, kind of, myself doing it, like, more, like, discerning that, actually, whether I like it or not, I cannot want things to be otherwise than they already are, because it's just me interfering with

212
00:24:19,795 --> 00:24:24,990
[SPEAKER_01]: What determines what I can interfere with, which is kind of a contradiction in itself.

213
00:24:29,282 --> 00:24:35,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it that, like, what would you practically discern?

214
00:24:35,228 --> 00:24:41,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's say, you have wanting of some even neutral kind.

215
00:24:42,314 --> 00:24:46,338
[SPEAKER_02]: How do you see that you cannot interfere?

216
00:24:46,418 --> 00:24:53,384
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, again, just telling yourself, like, I cannot interfere, it might be accurate, but unless you actually see

217
00:24:54,345 --> 00:25:00,411
[SPEAKER_03]: The fact that, like, you put... you see it in such a way that even if you wanted to interfere, you see that you can't.

218
00:25:01,432 --> 00:25:02,713
[SPEAKER_02]: How are you able to see that way?

219
00:25:05,436 --> 00:25:09,380
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you kind of have to develop the kind of recognitions that...

220
00:25:09,860 --> 00:25:17,428
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, one of the kind of foundation of mindfulness, for example, like just organizing that, well,

221
00:25:18,737 --> 00:25:23,619
[SPEAKER_01]: The body is like the basis on which I can act and I can never step outside of that, regardless.

222
00:25:24,340 --> 00:25:35,645
[SPEAKER_01]: And just like, not like putting it in the front, like, just like looking at looking at that, but just more recognizing, like, my whole experience is defined by that.

223
00:25:36,325 --> 00:25:38,827
[SPEAKER_01]: And I can never, like, actually get out of that.

224
00:25:38,927 --> 00:25:41,368
[SPEAKER_01]: As you said at the beginning, Patrick, like, say, like,

225
00:25:42,557 --> 00:25:45,379
[SPEAKER_01]: My experience as a whole is defined by that, regardless.

226
00:25:46,079 --> 00:25:48,301
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm just keeping that in mind, but let it sink.

227
00:25:48,801 --> 00:25:54,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, like, over-rationalizing it, but, like, just let it pervade, so to say.

228
00:25:54,384 --> 00:25:54,645
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

229
00:25:55,465 --> 00:26:07,032
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you basically have to recognize that even if you go and, like, you don't deny that whatever it is your mind wants to do, that you could do it.

230
00:26:07,993 --> 00:26:11,215
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that would be kind of just, um...

231
00:26:13,111 --> 00:26:14,652
[SPEAKER_02]: Wishful thinking, in a way.

232
00:26:14,712 --> 00:26:16,894
[SPEAKER_03]: No, it's like, there is a possibility there.

233
00:26:18,155 --> 00:26:26,042
[SPEAKER_03]: But the fact is, even if I were to actually go and do it, would I ever step outside of this foundation?

234
00:26:27,323 --> 00:26:28,264
[SPEAKER_03]: Actually, you realize, no.

235
00:26:28,424 --> 00:26:31,527
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, from that perspective,

236
00:26:33,229 --> 00:26:34,110
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you become clear.

237
00:26:34,510 --> 00:26:38,333
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, even when I... let's say you take the foundation of feeling.

238
00:26:39,074 --> 00:26:55,126
[SPEAKER_03]: Even when I actually went, and in the past, and engaged with sensuality, and pursued this, and got angry with people, and got into fights and stuff, did I ever actually step outside of that feeling that initially got me to act?

239
00:26:57,802 --> 00:26:58,943
[SPEAKER_03]: And you realize, actually, no.

240
00:26:59,203 --> 00:27:03,046
[SPEAKER_03]: It's, it's... pretty much every time, it actually even got worse.

241
00:27:04,246 --> 00:27:17,215
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you just recognize that, you see, okay, yes, I could act, like, I could go and do this, whatever, but, from this perspective, I'm not actually moving anywhere.

242
00:27:18,603 --> 00:27:20,405
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's when it ceases to be a saṅkhara.

243
00:27:20,625 --> 00:27:23,268
[SPEAKER_03]: I think we spoke about that at some point.

244
00:27:23,969 --> 00:27:29,955
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, it's a saṅkhara not because of the kind of movement of your arms or something.

245
00:27:30,476 --> 00:27:35,942
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a saṅkhara because there's that assumption that you are actually going somewhere else.

246
00:27:38,192 --> 00:27:48,121
[SPEAKER_03]: Whereas you realize, actually, no, there's anything that's done would have to remain exactly on the same level as where I started from.

247
00:27:48,941 --> 00:27:50,643
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, that's what I mean.

248
00:27:50,683 --> 00:27:55,207
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, even if you were to go and do it, you don't deny that there is that possibility.

249
00:27:55,767 --> 00:27:57,208
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, as far as you...

250
00:27:58,009 --> 00:27:59,210
[SPEAKER_03]: Possibly be able to go.

251
00:27:59,750 --> 00:28:01,572
[SPEAKER_03]: You will never be able to step outside.

252
00:28:01,932 --> 00:28:04,254
[SPEAKER_03]: You will never actually be able to go anywhere.

253
00:28:04,814 --> 00:28:19,226
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where that sutta, where the Buddha says, like, the the world is to be known within the confines of this phalanx-long body, and it's it's the cessation of the world is found there as well.

254
00:28:19,706 --> 00:28:20,467
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in other words,

255
00:28:21,843 --> 00:28:23,004
[SPEAKER_02]: There is nothing outside of it.

256
00:28:23,084 --> 00:28:26,608
[SPEAKER_03]: And anywhere that you would go, you still remain in that same body.

257
00:28:27,809 --> 00:28:37,500
[SPEAKER_03]: Like... and when you... as you said before, when you're daydreaming, when you're having kind of unwholesome thoughts even about this or that, instead of just

258
00:28:38,779 --> 00:29:04,592
[SPEAKER_03]: Replacing it with another kind of daydreaming, because that's a funny thing, like, you would kind of imply that if I'm focusing on some specific object, magically, that isn't daydreaming anymore, but actually, just because it's kind of more moving less or something, but it's exactly still the same assumption of movement, the same assumption of going somewhere else.

259
00:29:05,153 --> 00:29:05,573
[SPEAKER_02]: Instead,

260
00:29:07,095 --> 00:29:20,318
[SPEAKER_02]: You go the other way, and you recognize, you undermine the whole possibility, and you see, it doesn't matter what I do, it doesn't matter what I attend to, it doesn't matter whether I sit, stand, walk, or lie down, I'm not actually going anywhere.

261
00:29:21,238 --> 00:29:35,302
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's, that's, eh... in a way, that's, eh... probably even in a pleasant recognition, especially if you're not developed in virtue, because it's like being confined, and realizing, like, well,

262
00:29:37,180 --> 00:29:42,321
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, again, like, it's not even a matter of I am deciding to calm my mind or something.

263
00:29:42,622 --> 00:29:49,563
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, even if I were to ignore this and go back, or whatever, I won't ever...

264
00:29:50,144 --> 00:29:52,224
[SPEAKER_05]: I won't ever actually be able to get outside of this.

265
00:29:52,304 --> 00:29:53,965
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you're properly cornered, basically.

266
00:29:54,265 --> 00:29:54,585
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

267
00:29:56,946 --> 00:30:02,027
[SPEAKER_03]: But, at the same time, it is also the most relieving thing of all, because you realize, like,

268
00:30:03,238 --> 00:30:07,622
[SPEAKER_03]: Also, I don't even have to do what I assumed the practice was.

269
00:30:09,463 --> 00:30:15,188
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, go here, and do this, and think about this, and remove this, or do that.

270
00:30:16,469 --> 00:30:17,971
[SPEAKER_02]: No, it's actually the other direction.

271
00:30:18,031 --> 00:30:23,415
[SPEAKER_02]: It's recognizing that you can't even change anything, even if you want to.

272
00:30:25,288 --> 00:30:43,158
[SPEAKER_02]: But before that, in order to arrive at that point, like I said before, you need to have tried and strived and done things on that level of virtue, restraint and stuff, because otherwise you would just be misconceiving what saṅkharas are based on

273
00:30:43,938 --> 00:30:51,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever assumption can be used, you carry, and you won't be seeing it concreting in that yoniso sense.

274
00:30:51,881 --> 00:30:55,003
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, right here, what am I actually doing?

275
00:30:55,563 --> 00:31:05,927
[SPEAKER_02]: You instead be imposing whatever you read, or heard, or thought, or reflected saṅkharas are into your experience, and then that's... anything that comes out of that is just going to be another saṅkhara.

276
00:31:17,904 --> 00:31:21,332
[SPEAKER_00]: I think even, yeah, actually, we'll give you a simile of, like, um...

277
00:31:23,875 --> 00:31:27,957
[SPEAKER_00]: When he referred to... that's the word that people translate as, like, how to stop thinking.

278
00:31:28,777 --> 00:31:29,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Majjhima 20.

279
00:31:30,278 --> 00:31:37,381
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, eh... how do you calm the activity, the saṅkhara, of thinking.

280
00:31:38,122 --> 00:31:45,025
[SPEAKER_02]: And he said, eh... it's like a person who's running, recognizes, why am I running?

281
00:31:45,685 --> 00:31:46,406
[SPEAKER_02]: Why if I walk?

282
00:31:47,106 --> 00:31:50,207
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, after walking for a while, they recognize, why am I walking?

283
00:31:50,247 --> 00:31:51,128
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do I stand?

284
00:31:52,351 --> 00:31:55,695
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, it goes all the way down to, okay, so why am I sitting?

285
00:31:56,596 --> 00:31:58,158
[SPEAKER_03]: Let me lie down.

286
00:31:58,198 --> 00:32:06,788
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, you realize the whole principle there is that the saṅkhara is something that the person becomes aware of, like they were previously unaware of.

287
00:32:06,808 --> 00:32:08,690
[SPEAKER_02]: They weren't realizing it.

288
00:32:08,850 --> 00:32:12,995
[SPEAKER_02]: Once they recognize it, they're able to kind of

289
00:32:14,927 --> 00:32:20,213
[SPEAKER_02]: If you don't recognize it, if you don't see exactly what it is that

290
00:32:31,611 --> 00:32:40,199
[SPEAKER_02]: If you don't see that the whole thing is actually coming from you, it's not being imposed by your mind, kind of working on its own or something.

291
00:32:40,279 --> 00:32:45,725
[SPEAKER_02]: It's you choosing to engage with these things that present themselves.

292
00:32:47,196 --> 00:32:48,797
[SPEAKER_02]: And nobody else can come in between.

293
00:32:48,837 --> 00:32:49,718
[SPEAKER_02]: It's you alone.

294
00:32:50,078 --> 00:32:58,643
[SPEAKER_02]: If you don't see that, anything you try to do is still going to be within the same cragons, in ignorance as well, yeah.

295
00:32:59,344 --> 00:33:09,750
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you say, like, now is like, as long as someone doesn't recognize, like, this principle, like, to the full extent, he basically didn't take dukkha responsibility fully, yes?

296
00:33:10,718 --> 00:33:12,639
[SPEAKER_01]: Still, kind of, assume that something happened.

297
00:33:12,739 --> 00:33:13,720
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

298
00:33:13,900 --> 00:33:24,145
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's... like, that is why it is fully... like, nothing can... like, fully universal.

299
00:33:24,725 --> 00:33:36,831
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if it were that something you kind of concoct and it just came up, you develop this thing and then it sort of stays there for a while, inherently, that's going to be impermanent.

300
00:33:37,592 --> 00:33:40,556
[SPEAKER_02]: But if it's you recognizing, oh, I was actually doing this stuff.

301
00:33:41,277 --> 00:33:41,958
[SPEAKER_02]: Let me now stop.

302
00:33:43,520 --> 00:33:44,521
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, who can actually...

303
00:33:45,482 --> 00:33:52,331
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if, like, let's say you were running and somebody puts a gun to your head and says, start running again.

304
00:33:53,072 --> 00:33:54,914
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, he can't actually force you to run.

305
00:33:55,759 --> 00:34:00,703
[SPEAKER_02]: It would be you who would make a choice not to die and run instead.

306
00:34:01,283 --> 00:34:03,805
[SPEAKER_02]: So, nobody can force you to do anything.

307
00:34:03,825 --> 00:34:10,770
[SPEAKER_03]: And in the same way, nobody can force you to engage or to do saṅkharas.

308
00:34:10,970 --> 00:34:12,571
[SPEAKER_03]: Nobody can force you to have craving.

309
00:34:14,983 --> 00:34:25,308
[SPEAKER_02]: Not even Mara, as the Sutta said, like, when he was trying to provoke the Buddha, because he said, well, I own the eye, I own the ear, I own the... all of the six senses.

310
00:34:26,048 --> 00:34:27,349
[SPEAKER_02]: And then the Buddha said, yes, that's true.

311
00:34:28,409 --> 00:34:28,729
[SPEAKER_03]: But...

312
00:34:29,109 --> 00:34:32,151
[SPEAKER_03]: He didn't say, he didn't say, oh, you're wrong about that.

313
00:34:32,271 --> 00:34:34,012
[SPEAKER_03]: He said, no, that's factually correct.

314
00:34:34,932 --> 00:34:38,614
[SPEAKER_03]: But there is an escape from that, which you don't understand.

315
00:34:44,272 --> 00:34:47,231
[SPEAKER_05]: Now, is taking responsibility an activity?

316
00:35:13,266 --> 00:35:13,766
[SPEAKER_04]: It's a thought.

317
00:35:13,947 --> 00:35:15,027
[SPEAKER_05]: So, I think, yes.

318
00:35:17,909 --> 00:35:21,612
[SPEAKER_05]: So, is that something that, in the end, can also go to the cessation of saṅkhara?

319
00:35:25,475 --> 00:35:28,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because I think there is a difference in between, like, taking responsibility

320
00:35:32,377 --> 00:35:37,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Taking already implies something you did.

321
00:35:38,480 --> 00:35:39,261
[SPEAKER_03]: The verb taking.

322
00:35:39,801 --> 00:35:48,006
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you realize, well, I am responsible, like the same principle, I am responsible even if I don't want to be responsible, then it's not you taking responsibility.

323
00:35:48,066 --> 00:35:49,387
[SPEAKER_02]: And ironically, that recognition

324
00:35:50,307 --> 00:35:52,988
[SPEAKER_02]: It frees you from the burden of responsibility.

325
00:35:53,508 --> 00:35:59,811
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the only reason responsibility was a burden is because you thought, you still implied that it was possible to avoid it.

326
00:36:02,052 --> 00:36:05,553
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the principle with everything.

327
00:36:05,573 --> 00:36:09,695
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you don't actually fix what you think the problem is.

328
00:36:10,555 --> 00:36:14,037
[SPEAKER_02]: You simply realize that there was no need to fix it in the first place.

329
00:36:15,622 --> 00:36:44,216
[SPEAKER_00]: In terms of getting rid of pain and getting rid of... or pursuing pleasure, of course, initially, like I said, it is a problem if you're engaging in certain actions, like breaking the precept, because as much as you might want to tell yourself, through fancy ideas, that you can do that without craving, you are actually taking up the entire spectrum of experience just by doing any of those things.

330
00:36:57,409 --> 00:37:00,236
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what would be another very...

331
00:37:01,830 --> 00:37:27,942
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a pertinent saṅkhara that would be there when you give up, let's say, the obvious bad saṅkharas of... let's say you... the person is taking the precepts and bodily, verbally and even mentally, they don't engage with sensuality, aversion or distraction.

332
00:37:29,902 --> 00:37:39,167
[SPEAKER_00]: That, of course, doesn't mean that they're pure already, because then it would take one day to turn around.

333
00:37:40,287 --> 00:37:41,248
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is left there?

334
00:37:42,348 --> 00:37:46,951
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still not quite reined in.

335
00:37:53,934 --> 00:37:55,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Isn't it like the same principle?

336
00:37:55,315 --> 00:37:56,636
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I am doing all this.

337
00:37:57,437 --> 00:38:04,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I am the one, like, holding this precept, holding this virtue, holding this sense-restraint, and there will be a result out of it.

338
00:38:04,519 --> 00:38:06,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I'm expecting these things.

339
00:38:06,980 --> 00:38:09,700
[SPEAKER_03]: That is... that is the... what it kind of boils down to.

340
00:38:09,720 --> 00:38:25,585
[SPEAKER_02]: But, more specifically, what... what is the kind of... what would be the thing that you're still doing when, merely having restrained yourself from coarse behaviors, you'd still be kind of...

341
00:38:28,334 --> 00:38:31,116
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, there would be hindrances, for example.

342
00:38:31,776 --> 00:38:31,997
[SPEAKER_05]: Sure.

343
00:38:32,477 --> 00:38:36,620
[SPEAKER_05]: Or just the expectation that there's still something to get that you do not have.

344
00:38:36,780 --> 00:38:37,620
[SPEAKER_05]: Right, yeah, exactly.

345
00:38:37,680 --> 00:38:40,502
[SPEAKER_05]: But what is it that you would be trying to get that you don't have?

346
00:38:43,464 --> 00:38:48,727
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, you think that you can get peace if the feeling which is not present is otherwise, for example.

347
00:38:49,628 --> 00:38:51,009
[SPEAKER_03]: That's basically, yeah.

348
00:38:51,149 --> 00:38:53,911
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then, you realize

349
00:38:55,592 --> 00:39:05,740
[SPEAKER_03]: Or still, you would still carry the assumption that the solution to all of this is to replace whatever pressure is there with something else.

350
00:39:05,880 --> 00:39:06,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

351
00:39:06,981 --> 00:39:10,784
[SPEAKER_00]: And for that reason alone, you're suffering.

352
00:39:10,804 --> 00:39:11,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Like it's the craving.

353
00:39:12,925 --> 00:39:15,648
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not what you thought you had to resolve.

354
00:39:15,708 --> 00:39:16,568
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always the craving.

355
00:39:17,449 --> 00:39:23,534
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why the Buddha said, even in the, like in the satipaṭṭhāna contemplation,

356
00:39:24,977 --> 00:39:33,004
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, when there's a mind with greed, he said, you know the mind of greed is a mind with greed.

357
00:39:34,125 --> 00:39:39,430
[SPEAKER_02]: And it says, through that practice, you avoid disengaged, not taking up anything in the world.

358
00:39:40,610 --> 00:39:46,654
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't say that he replaces the mind of greed with something else.

359
00:39:47,275 --> 00:39:50,037
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, again, that would be taking it up.

360
00:39:50,137 --> 00:39:56,141
[SPEAKER_02]: You abandon the mind of greed simply to the extent that you don't act out of it.

361
00:39:57,462 --> 00:40:06,107
[SPEAKER_03]: But then, now, if you also carry the opposite activity of still, to some tiny extent, expecting that it shouldn't be there...

362
00:40:06,127 --> 00:40:07,768
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is part of the greed, actually.

363
00:40:08,048 --> 00:40:08,548
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

364
00:40:08,568 --> 00:40:09,809
[SPEAKER_03]: That partakes in the same thing.

365
00:40:10,049 --> 00:40:10,249
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

366
00:40:10,709 --> 00:40:15,532
[SPEAKER_03]: And, ironically, then, through the craving... Like, it's because of the craving that that greed is there.

367
00:40:16,652 --> 00:40:21,335
[SPEAKER_02]: So, by fueling the craving, whether it's towards or against, you're fueling the basis for greed.

368
00:40:22,095 --> 00:40:24,517
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, even if you did, actually,

369
00:40:25,707 --> 00:40:29,109
[SPEAKER_02]: Get rid of it somehow, through whatever means.

370
00:40:30,490 --> 00:40:36,253
[SPEAKER_02]: The fact that you still cultivated craving, that's precisely what's going to make it arise again.

371
00:40:37,614 --> 00:40:43,877
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if, oh, you feel victorious or whatever, because you succeeded in removing it.

372
00:40:44,197 --> 00:40:46,979
[SPEAKER_02]: That's still pretty much the same principle of sensuality.

373
00:40:47,079 --> 00:40:54,883
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, I just have this thing, push it away with something better, and feel satisfied as a result of that.

374
00:41:01,077 --> 00:41:10,565
[SPEAKER_02]: It is quite a hard thing for people to accept, because all their views are about countering and finding a quick solution for the problem.

375
00:41:11,746 --> 00:41:25,496
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you don't realize, actually, that middle way that the Buddha described is a much more subtle thing than just like, oh, don't be too kind of ascetic and don't be too indulgent either.

376
00:41:25,836 --> 00:41:26,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, that's the

377
00:41:28,603 --> 00:41:33,669
[SPEAKER_00]: Broad kind of principle that you'd be able to recognize externally, but even that...

378
00:41:34,611 --> 00:41:43,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the Buddha said... well, some of his disciples would be eating once every three days, and the other ones would be eating food from the king every day.

379
00:41:44,721 --> 00:41:47,483
[SPEAKER_00]: So... and wearing silk robe and whatnot.

380
00:41:47,503 --> 00:41:57,653
[SPEAKER_03]: So, even on that level, you can't quite say that it's just a external, predefined type of conduct.

381
00:41:58,113 --> 00:41:58,433
[SPEAKER_03]: It is...

382
00:41:59,334 --> 00:42:09,400
[SPEAKER_03]: Most securely, on that internal level, whether you kind of engage with the bad saṅkharas, or you engage with the good ones.

383
00:42:10,221 --> 00:42:21,668
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is, the good saṅkhara is the saṅkhara of trying to get, basically trying to get rid of your sensuality, trying to get rid of your aversion, through that sense of like, I want something else, I want this to go away.

384
00:42:21,688 --> 00:42:23,669
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, which eventually also has to cease.

385
00:42:23,729 --> 00:42:24,250
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, yeah.

386
00:42:24,650 --> 00:42:25,971
[SPEAKER_03]: So that's the, that's the

387
00:42:28,425 --> 00:42:36,450
[SPEAKER_00]: All of the times the Buddha would describe, like, oh, the tuning of the strings, how do you cross the flood?

388
00:42:36,650 --> 00:42:39,572
[SPEAKER_02]: It's by not pushing forward, by not standing still.

389
00:42:40,292 --> 00:42:42,213
[SPEAKER_03]: So, all of that converges around that principle.

390
00:42:42,253 --> 00:42:44,175
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you push forward, well...

391
00:42:46,696 --> 00:42:48,457
[SPEAKER_03]: Initially, let's say, you're not even pushing forward.

392
00:42:48,477 --> 00:42:51,859
[SPEAKER_03]: You're just letting it carry you away.

393
00:42:52,559 --> 00:42:56,321
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's your initial state of everybody as a householder.

394
00:42:56,401 --> 00:42:57,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Just give in.

395
00:42:57,982 --> 00:42:58,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Just go with the flow.

396
00:42:58,822 --> 00:42:59,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Just go with it.

397
00:43:00,423 --> 00:43:07,707
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, of course, unless you fight it, you won't be able to establish the basis for the middle way, which is the precepts.

398
00:43:08,347 --> 00:43:19,192
[SPEAKER_03]: But at a certain point, you have to realize, wait, now I need to learn how to drop this without going back to just not doing anything at all and letting the flood carry me.

399
00:43:19,872 --> 00:43:24,935
[SPEAKER_00]: So, without doing either, that's the cessation of suffering.

400
00:43:51,862 --> 00:43:54,083
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's basically what sīlavata paramāsa is.

401
00:43:55,104 --> 00:44:01,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's not a thing that's just hidden somewhere in your subconscious.

402
00:44:03,049 --> 00:44:07,732
[SPEAKER_00]: It is like something that will actually be present quite frequently.

403
00:44:07,772 --> 00:44:20,020
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like that tendency to kind of literally hold on to the sense of duty, of like, I need to get rid of this, I need to kind of... just going beyond

404
00:44:21,647 --> 00:44:23,187
[SPEAKER_03]: You were at the right point.

405
00:44:23,207 --> 00:44:24,228
[SPEAKER_03]: You just gave up.

406
00:44:24,688 --> 00:44:31,850
[SPEAKER_03]: You stopped engaging with sensuality, but you keep carrying it further into, like, out of just that sense of duty.

407
00:44:31,950 --> 00:44:33,451
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't have to be anything specific.

408
00:44:34,111 --> 00:44:36,552
[SPEAKER_05]: That momentum has to cease as well, basically.

409
00:44:36,592 --> 00:44:36,832
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

410
00:44:36,972 --> 00:44:47,555
[SPEAKER_02]: Not discerning that, actually, I already did what I had to do, and then I just keep winding myself up by assuming that there's something more and then overlooking.

411
00:44:48,143 --> 00:44:54,767
[SPEAKER_03]: Just by overlooking the fact that, oh, I was actually right where I had to be, then you just keep going elsewhere.

412
00:44:54,908 --> 00:44:58,690
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, because probably it felt sort of dissatisfying or unpleasant.

413
00:44:58,730 --> 00:44:58,870
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

414
00:44:58,930 --> 00:44:59,991
[SPEAKER_05]: I think there's more to be done.

415
00:45:00,071 --> 00:45:00,291
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

416
00:45:00,451 --> 00:45:03,633
[SPEAKER_03]: Rather than to just... And that goes back to what I said.

417
00:45:03,673 --> 00:45:10,078
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, any sense of what you might assume is what you need to resolve,

418
00:45:11,137 --> 00:45:17,902
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just going to be a distraction from what the actual problem is, which is, right now, I'm craving against something.

419
00:45:20,183 --> 00:45:24,426
[SPEAKER_02]: If there's any sense of, like, oh, doubt and asking myself, oh, what is it?

420
00:45:24,466 --> 00:45:25,046
[SPEAKER_02]: What is it?

421
00:45:25,206 --> 00:45:27,188
[SPEAKER_02]: And feeling overwhelmed or something.

422
00:45:27,428 --> 00:45:32,211
[SPEAKER_02]: All of that can only be because I'm overlooking something that should, in a way, be obvious.

423
00:45:33,572 --> 00:45:37,815
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not because of the kind of built-up ignorance, but you realize, actually, it's right there.

424
00:45:39,435 --> 00:45:46,179
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not as, kind of, clearly defined as what I'm used to seeing, like objects out there in the world.

425
00:45:46,639 --> 00:45:49,400
[SPEAKER_02]: But it is, factually, I am feeling like this.

426
00:45:49,460 --> 00:45:51,741
[SPEAKER_02]: If somebody were to come and ask me, like, how do you feel?

427
00:45:52,422 --> 00:45:53,602
[SPEAKER_02]: You would give the answer.

428
00:45:54,282 --> 00:45:59,625
[SPEAKER_02]: And right there, in relation to that ambiguous feeling, you are trying to get rid of it.

429
00:45:59,665 --> 00:46:00,485
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the problem.

430
00:46:00,645 --> 00:46:03,807
[SPEAKER_00]: There cannot be anything else that causes you suffering.

431
00:46:12,161 --> 00:46:20,685
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's just drilling that in, because you may recognize it for a moment, but then the assumption will set back in, and you will continue then trying to...

432
00:46:21,240 --> 00:46:22,701
[SPEAKER_02]: Find the answer and resolve it.

433
00:46:22,781 --> 00:46:31,905
[SPEAKER_02]: And somehow, through different ways, it's not always going to be the same solution that you're going to be striving for.

434
00:46:32,525 --> 00:46:34,426
[SPEAKER_02]: But the common thread is you're looking for something.

435
00:46:34,566 --> 00:46:35,326
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the problem.

436
00:46:35,486 --> 00:46:39,348
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter whether you incline up, down, left, right, front or back.

437
00:46:39,628 --> 00:46:46,811
[SPEAKER_02]: The point is that inclination, in and of itself, is what causes birth, aging, death, all of what I said before.

